





cache the SI template + symbolic Jacobian per cell (refactor — high impact)
    -- Symptom: hard cells (crauste at 1em8, biohydrogenation at 1em6,
       similar low-noise multipoint-heavy cases) rebuild the SI template
       from scratch hundreds of times per cell. Measured on wallaby 2026-05-17:
         * biohydrogenation_3_1em6 nopolish: 78 instances of
           "[DEBUG-SI-VARS] Final variables list (39)" in stderr — i.e.
           the full symbolic template was instantiated 78 times for the
           same (39 eq, 39 var) system.
         * crauste_2_1em4 nopolish: 222 "Computing IO-equations", 220
           "Getting equation system from SI", 1543 "Floating point value
           0.5 will be converted to 1//2" warnings from rational
           conversion. (Stderr is in
           <PEB>/benchmark_wallaby_2026-05-17/filetree/odepe_v2_nopolish_run/
           {biohydrogenation_3_1em6,crauste_2_1em4}/stderr.txt for now —
           they may rotate.)
         * crauste_3_1em8 nopolish OOM-killed at MaxRSS = 8.36 GB after
           16h 54min of wall (sacct: 67613_283, ExitCode 0:125). Same
           cell's polish run finished in 1h54min at 5.5 GB. The wall-time
           and memory gap is the template-rebuild loop, not the polish step
           itself.

    -- What's actually invariant per cell:
         1. The SI template's variable list (the `Any[M_0, L_0, ...]`
            symbol vector). Function of model + measured quantities only.
         2. The symbolic Jacobian of that template (the
            "[ROBUST] Building symbolic Jacobian..." step). Same inputs.
       What varies per shooting point / per multipoint pair / per
       aggregate-candidate restart:
         3. The numeric polynomial-instance coefficients (data values
            plugged into the symbolic template).
       Only (3) needs to be redone. The current code re-derives (1) and
       (2) every iteration of the resolve loop.

    -- Memory mechanism: each symbolic rebuild allocates fresh Term/Sym
       objects into the global Symbolics.jl intern table; those don't
       reliably GC because the table holds strong refs. 200+ rebuilds
       → linear growth in the intern table → eventual OOM on long-wall
       hard cells. Polish doesn't OOM as readily because LM termination
       caps the outer loop earlier.

    -- Proposed refactor (concrete-ish):
         * Build SI template + symbolic Jacobian ONCE per cell at the
           top of `process_raw_solution` / wherever
           `Building symbolic Jacobian` is currently emitted (search:
           `[ROBUST] Building symbolic Jacobian` in source).
         * Cache the result under a key derived from the SI template
           signature: (sorted variable list, sorted equation polynomial
           hashes). This handles the rare case where rank trimming changes
           the active equation set between resolve iterations.
         * For each shooting point / multipoint pair, substitute the new
           numeric coefficients into the cached symbolic structure rather
           than re-deriving. If the cache key changes (e.g. a rank-deficient
           shooting point produces a different reduced template), fall back
           to a fresh build for that iteration only.
         * Guard with explicit `Symbolics.unwrap_freevars()` or
           `Base.finalize` after the cell completes, to release the intern
           table at end-of-cell rather than at end-of-Julia-process.

    -- Why this is "reusable in principle":
       The math is unchanged. Per-iteration we still get the same set of
       solutions to the same numeric polynomial system; we just don't pay
       the symbolic-derivation cost again. The "[ROBUST]" annotation in
       the current path is defensive against the rank-collapse edge case;
       a cache-with-fallback preserves the defense while paying the symbolic
       cost once instead of N times.

    -- Expected impact:
         * Memory: nopolish on crauste/bioh-at-low-noise should drop from
           5-8 GB peak to ~1-2 GB peak. Fits in 4 GB SLURM allocations,
           removes the OOM class entirely.
         * Wall time: nopolish hard cells should drop 5-10× (the
           symbolic rebuild is dominating wall, not the numeric solve).
           crauste_3_1em8 might go from 17 hr → 2-3 hr.
         * Polish: smaller win (polish's outer loop terminates faster
           already), but probably 1.5-3× faster on hard cells.

    -- Risks / caveats:
         * The cache key needs to capture more than just var list if
           equation rank-trimming or aggregate-candidate generation can
           swap in different polynomial equations. Tests against
           crauste/biohydrogenation/daisy_mamil4 cells across all 5 noise
           levels would be the minimum acceptance gate.
         * If the wallaby polish regressions (see
           <PEB>/results/wallaby_analysis/INVESTIGATION_polish_regression_root_cause.md)
           turn out to be OrdinaryDiffEq-7.0.0-related, the same fix may
           uncover or fix subtle convergence dependencies in the polish
           pipeline. Worth re-baselining wallaby after this lands.

    -- Files most likely involved (search and verify):
         * src/core/parameter_estimation.jl (resolve loops, multipoint
           iteration, aggregate-candidate generation)
         * src/core/analysis_utils.jl (template instantiation,
           synthesize_aggregate_candidates)
         * Wherever "[ROBUST] Building symbolic Jacobian" is emitted —
           this is the actual hot path.

